illion\\\" in t))):\n    print(e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"publication_date\\\"],e[\\\"input_id\\\"],e[\\\"thesis_ids\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"][:320])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-2 Q5 v5 gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-09-19 1856 gpt-6-luna 7106 3589 1167 0.00240007\nold-2 Q40 v40 gnews:4dca50e22bd9f158d2a04186 2026-09-20 1884 gpt-6-luna 3740 2305 1167 0.00142147\nold-3 Q6 v6 gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-09-19 1856 gpt-6-luna 7783 3149 1167 0.00224777\nold-3 Q47 v47 gnews:4dca50e22bd9f158d2a04186 2026-09-20 1884 gpt-6-luna 3424 1086 1167 0.00078037\nfour cost 0.0068496799999999995 eight est 0.013699359999999999\nnew-2\n208:ev-2 2026-07-13 gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 [] Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks costs between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks; under Vera Rubin, rack costs rise to $25 billion. The article separately states that Broadcom a\n1680:ev4 2026-09-14 gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb ['clean-5'] The article states that the four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n982:ev-5 2026-08-23 gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 ['clean-5'] The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion of capital expenditure in 2026 alone, according to Fool.com — the aggregate hyperscaler capex pool that underpins data-center accelerator demand.\n1423:e4 2026-09-04 gnews:bbd72d5fbff98d39746e6cb9 ['clean-5'] CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA can reach $40 billion of revenue per gigawatt of AI computing capacity with Vera Rubin, versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell and about $18 billion per gigawatt with Hopper, as NVIDIA sells more of the complete rack (accelerators, networking and its own processors).\n1616:e9 2026-09-12 gnews:0c19765ee99d1f808e0834ec ['clean-51'] Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said on the company's late-August 2026 earnings call that a gigawatt of its older Hopper systems meant about $18 billion of revenue opportunity for Nvidia, that Blackwell runs about $25 billion per gigawatt, and that the new Vera Rubin platform is about $40 billion per gigaw\nnew-3\n1680:e4 2026-09-14 gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb ['clean-4'] Zacks reports that the four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n982:e5 2026-08-23 gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 ['clean-4'] The four largest AI hyperscalers plan $760 billion of capital expenditure in calendar 2026, according to the Motley Fool analyst, who cites the figure as the basis for investor worry about how long the AI build-out can keep growing.\n1101:e18 2026-08-26 gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 [] NVIDIA estimated its revenue opportunity at roughly $18 billion per gigawatt of data center capacity in the Hopper generation, $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, including CPUs, GPUs, networking, systems and software.\n1423:e4 2026-09-04 gnews:bbd72d5fbff98d39746e6cb9 [] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, as reported, quantified the company's revenue per gigawatt of installed computing capacity: about $18 billion per gigawatt with Hopper, rising to $25 billion with Blackwell, and reaching $40 billion per gigawatt with Vera Rubin — the increase coming from selling more of the AI rack (accelerato\n1586:ev-5 2026-09-11 gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 ['clean-2'] Nvidia estimates a $40 billion revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI compute with its Vera Rubin family of processors, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with its Blackwell family, as reported from CEO Jensen Huang's Q2 FY2027 commentary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nimport json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17\\\")\nr=json.loads((p/\\\"reading-diagnostic/deck/readings.json\\\").read_text())\nold={\\\"M04\\\",\\\"M06\\\",\\\"M10\\\",\\\"M11\\\",\\\"M13\\\",\\\"M15\\\",\\\"M17\\\",\\\"M18\\\",\\\"M19\\\",\\\"M31\\\"}\nstrict={\\\"M13\\\",\\\"M15\\\"};lenient=strict|{\\\"M31\\\"}\nown={x[\\\"matter\\\"] for x in r[\\\"matters\\\"] if x[\\\"company_figure\\\"]}\nfor new in (strict,lenient):\n for elig in (old-own,old):\n  h=elig|new; a=len(h-old);b=len(old-h)\n  print(\\\"bound\\\",len(h),a,b,\\\"new-only needed\\\",max(b+5,2*b), \\\"excluded\\\",sorted(old-h))\nfor d,items in ((\\\"old-2\\\",(\\\"v5\\\",\\\"v40\\\")),(\\\"old-3\\\",(\\\"v6\\\",\\\"v47\\\"))):\n db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/f\\\"run/{d}/prod/views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\n for v in items:\n  print(d,v,db.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall())\nfor d,ids in ((\\\"new-2\\\",(\\\"1680:ev4\\\",\\\"982:ev-5\\\",\\\"1694:ev-1\\\",\\\"1423:e4\\\")),(\\\"new-3\\\",(\\\"1680:e4\\\",\\\"982:e5\\\",\\\"1694:e1\\\",\\\"1423:e4\\\"))):\n l=json.loads((p/f\\\"run/{d}/step2/library.json\\\").read_text())\n db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/f\\\"run/{d}/step1/trace.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\n items=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"items-comparison.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\n for wanted in ids:\n  g=l[\\\"groups\\\"][l[\\\"placement\\\"][wanted]]\n  e=next(x for x in [g[\\\"original\\\"],*g[\\\"members\\\"]] if x[\\\"id\\\"]==wanted)\n  traces=db.execute(\\\"select request,raw_response,result from trace where input_id=?\\\",(e[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()\n  assert traces\n  text=items.execute(\\\"select text,text_article,text_page from items where input_id=?\\\",(e[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()\n  print(d,wanted,e[\\\"publication_date\\\"],\\\"verified\\\", [s[:250] for s in e[\\\"verified_passages\\\"] if s],\\\"trace\\\",all(t is not None for t in traces),\\\"in raw source\\\",any(s in t for s in e[\\\"verified_passages\\\"] if s for t in text if t))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
